Osteoarthritis (Base of CMCJ)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Base of thumb pain with pinch, Positive CMC grind test, Joint base squaring

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-036
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, sharp thumb pain rendering the patient unable to use the dominant hand for essential tasks like turning keys or opening jars.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an elderly patient experiencing an acute, severe flare of localized thumb base pain, resulting in sudden, profound grip weakness and inability to perform basic self-care and domestic tasks safely. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute joint infection or acute scaphoid fracture, and to provide immediate mechanical splinting and targeted analgesia to restore basic functional capacity prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The base of my thumb is killing me; I tried to unlock my front door this evening and the pain was so sharp I almost dropped my keys."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute-on-chronic exacerbation of right thumb base pain. Timeline: Long-standing history of deep, aching pain localized to the base of the thumb at the trapeziometacarpal joint for several years; typically associated with brief morning stiffness resolving within 30 minutes. Today, following a period of repetitive forceful gripping (gardening), the patient developed a severe, sharp pain flare. Exacerbation of discomfort is now extreme during any activities requiring a strong pinch grip. Patient reports progressive weakness in pinch strength secondary to pain, making it difficult to hold objects securely.
Functional Status: Impaired fine motor and pinch grip function; unable to safely manipulate small objects or perform tasks requiring torque (e.g., turning doorknobs).
Social/Safety Concerns: Functional limitation impacting independent living and self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 146/88 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, frustrated, actively avoiding use of the right thumb.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right hand reveals localized, hard, bony swelling and a distinct squared-off appearance at the base of the thumb (first carpometacarpal joint). Palpable, coarse crepitus is felt during active circumduction of the first metacarpal. Distinctly positive thumb grind test: axial compression and rotation of the CMC joint elicits sharp, reproducible pain and palpable crepitus. No overlying erythema, warmth, or soft, fluctuant synovial swelling to suggest acute gout or infection. Sensation in the median and radial nerve distributions is intact. Pinch grip strength is markedly reduced (3/5) due to pain inhibition.
Environmental Observations: Patient unable to open standard medication bottles due to pinch grip failure.
Medication Reconciliation: Took regular paracetamol with minimal effect on the acute mechanical pain flare.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute mechanical flare of First Carpometacarpal (CMC) Joint Osteoarthritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

De Quervain's Tenosynovitis - Ruled out by maximal tenderness being precisely at the CMC joint line rather than the radial styloid, and the positive grind test (articular pain) rather than primarily positive Finkelstein's test (tendon stretch pain).

Scaphoid Fracture - Ruled out by the absence of acute trauma and the classic chronic, progressive history with bony squaring.

Gout (Pseudogout) - Ruled out clinically by the lack of hyperacute onset, absence of intense erythema/heat, and the presence of chronic hard bony changes rather than soft tissue tophi.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, functionally limiting exacerbation of established base-of-thumb osteoarthritis, triggered by mechanical overload. The key clinical distinction was differentiating this acute mechanical flare from an inflammatory arthropathy or acute tendon rupture. Physical examination clearly localized the pathology to the articular surface of the CMC joint (positive grind test, bony squaring, crepitus). The sudden inability to perform a pinch grip rendered the patient functionally unsafe for basic ADLs. Postponing evaluation would have left the patient in severe pain and unable to manage their home environment. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the mechanical nature of the pain, ruled out infection, and allowed for the immediate application of a rigid splint to offload the joint and restore basic functional capacity.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Diclofenac gel to be applied strictly to the thumb base QID. Advised a short 3-day course of oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food to manage the acute joint inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Wear the provided rigid thumb-spica splint continuously for all gripping and pinching tasks over the next week. Consequence: The splint physically absorbs the mechanical torque that normally grinds the arthritic joint surfaces together, allowing the patient to turn keys and open jars without the sudden, sharp, agonizing pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Soak the hand in warm water for 10 minutes every morning before starting daily activities. Consequence: Increased local blood flow softens the stiff joint capsule, reducing the severe morning stiffness and making initial hand movements significantly easier (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized Hand Therapist via the primary GP.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a rigid thermoplastic thumb-spica splint to the right hand.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks to discuss long-term management, including potential intra-articular corticosteroid injection or surgical referral if conservative measures fail.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the joint suddenly becomes intensely hot, red, and swollen like a balloon, or if the patient develops a high fever and the shivers.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of joint wear and tear. X-rays can confirm the extent of cartilage loss but do not change immediate management. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain will settle over a week with the splint and anti-inflammatories, returning to the baseline dull ache. The bony bump is permanent.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the base of your thumb suddenly becomes bright red, hot to the touch, and massively swollen, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The cartilage pad at the base of your thumb has worn thin, causing the bones to grind against each other (crepitus) during forceful pinching. The gardening today caused the joint to become acutely inflamed. The rigid splint is mandatory; it acts as an external skeleton, taking the pressure off the grinding bones so the joint can rest.
Actionable Steps: You must wear the splint for any activity requiring a pinch or twist (keys, jars, dressing). Use the anti-inflammatory gel directly on the bony bump to calm the chemical pain signals locally.

